ay allow Rh1 to develop or prepare f3 to challenge the knight.\n\nAlternatively, moving to f1 connects with the h1 rook, but is more passive.\n\nd1 is toward the a1 rook, but again, less connected.\n\nBest practical choice: **e1-e2**, centralizing the king and allowing future development.\n\nBut let\'s double-check: is the knight really on g2?\n\nBoard says:  \nRow 2: ... |PWF0|NBF1|PWF0| — so g2 has NBF1 — Black Knight.\n\nAnd no white piece has captured — so yes, Black knight captured on g2, delivering check.\n\nWhite must move the king.\n\nAll four moves are legal escapes.\n\nAmong them, **K: e1-e2** is the most natural — it\'s a common way to bring the king to safety when castling isn\'t possible, and it doesn\'t block key files.\n\nReason: Moving the king to e2 provides a safe, centralized position, avoids blocking key rook files (like a1 or h1), and prepares for potential counterplay in the center or kingside development.\n\nAnswer: K: e1-e2'}
